/38435"} "Ercole Olgeni"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/38482"} "Giovanni Scatturin"] " of Italy were back to defend the crown that they had earned in Amsterdam, with a new partner, " [:a {:href "/athletes/38497"} "Gino Sopracordevole"] ". Bronze medalists " [:a {:href "/athletes/39708"} "Édouard Candeveau"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/39714"} "Alfred Felber"] " of Switzerland brought " [:a {:href "/athletes/39757"} "Émile Lachapelle"] " to try for gold once more. This trio had been successful previously, winning the 1922 and 1923 European Championships. Among five nations, the only non-European entry came from the United States, with the somewhat inexperienced crew of " [:a {:href "/athletes/40570"} "Leon Butler"] ", " [:a {:href "/athletes/40935"} "Harold Wilson"] ", and " [:a {:href "/athletes/40722"} "Ed Jennings"] ". Compared to the strong French trio of " [:a {:href "/athletes/36909"} "Eugène Constant"] ", " [:a {:href "/athletes/37108"} "Raymond Talleux"] ", and " [:a {:href "/athletes/37015"} "Marcel Lepan"] ", most observers felt that neither the Americans nor the Belgians would be a factor in this five-nation contest."] "\n\n" [:p {} "In the heats, France had the fastest time by almost 4.5 seconds, but the Americans surprised everyone by coming in second. Only Belgium failed to make the final, which was thought to be a shoo-in for France but, after leading for the first three-quarters of the race, they suddenly tired and allowed the others to pass. The competition came down to a neck and neck effort between the Italians and the Swiss, which the latter won by 0.1 seconds. The United States captured a surprise bronze medal, on which Jennings would improve in " [:a {:href "/results/157903"} "1932"] " by winning gold in Los Angeles."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitors"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} "Switzerland"] [:td {